# Project Guide

## Validation

Use the project commands for normal validation:

- `just check` — formatting and Clippy
- `just test` — tests that do not require external services

Tests requiring PostgreSQL or an ephemeral Temporal server are excluded from the
default run. To run the full suite:

1. Start PostgreSQL with `just local-pg`.
2. Run `just test-core-all`.

Targeted `cargo test` filters are fine during development, but run the relevant
project-level validation before finishing.

## Architecture

The workspace is divided into:

- `crates/core` — domain logic, database access, and reusable application code
- `bin/{{ project-name | kebab_case }}` — API, CLI, worker, and runtime wiring
- `crates/core/migrations` — embedded SQLx migrations

Keep business and database logic in `crates/core`. Binary-crate handlers should
translate inputs and delegate to `crates/core` rather than query the database
directly.

## Database

- Put PostgreSQL helpers under `crates/core/src/sqlx_postgres`.
- Add timestamped SQL migrations under `crates/core/migrations`.
- Use bind parameters for runtime values.
- Use SeaQuery when query structure is genuinely dynamic.
- Treat each `AssertSqlSafe` conversion from generated SQL as an audited boundary.
  Never interpolate user input into SQL fragments.
- Reuse the existing connection, migration, transaction, and executor patterns
  before introducing new ones.

## Temporal

Use the native Temporal Rust SDK 1.1 macros and typed APIs. Existing examples live in:

- `crates/core/src/temporal/mod.rs` — clients, workflows, and activities
- `bin/{{ project-name | kebab_case }}/src/worker.rs` — worker registration
- `bin/{{ project-name | kebab_case }}/src/http.rs` and `src/mono.rs` — application
  integration

Keep workflow and activity definitions in `crates/core`. Add application
abstractions only when they provide a meaningful domain boundary; do not merely
rename or re-export SDK types.

Use client-owned policy enums from `temporalio_client` and construct SDK options
with their builders or defaults. Use `Runtime::from_current_tokio` inside an active
Tokio runtime. Keep experimental features disabled unless a workflow needs them.
